    3. demands\\n        4. a\\n        5. raise\\n        6. in\\n        7. tuna.\\n        8. It\\n        9. is\\n        10. the\\n        11. only\\n        12. employee\\n        13. I\\n        14. ever\\n        15. have.\\n        Perfect 15.\\n\\n        Wait, is \\\"ever have\\\" necessary?\\n        \\\"It is the only employee I have.\\\" (7 words). Total 14.\\n        Let\'s go with a simpler one to ensure safety on the count.\\n\\n        Draft:\\n        Cats think they own the house.\\n        They just don\'t pay the rent.\\n        1. Cats\\n        2. think\\n        3. they\\n        4. own\\n        5. the\\n        6. house.\\n        7. They\\n        8. just\\n        9. don\'t\\n        10. pay\\n        11. the\\n        12. rent.\\n        Total: 12 words. Safe.\\n\\n        Can I make it funnier?\\n        My cat sleeps sixteen hours a day.\\n        It has the perfect nine-to-five job.\\n        1. My\\n        2. cat\\n        3. sleeps\\n        4. sixteen\\n        5. hours\\n        6. a\\n        7. day.\\n        8. It\\n        9. has\\n        10. the\\n        11. perfect\\n        12. nine-to-five\\n        13. job.\\n        (13 words). \\\"nine-to-five\\\" counts as one or three? Usually hyphenated words count as one token\\/word in standard counting, but to be safe, let\'s avoid hyphens if ambiguous.\\n\\n        Let\'s go with the rent joke. It\'s a classic.\\n        Line 1: My cat thinks the house is his.\\n        Line 2: I guess he just pays in rent.\\n        1. My\\n        2. cat\\n        3. thinks\\n        4. the\\n        5. house\\n        6. is\\n        7. his.\\n        8. I\\n        9. guess\\n        10. he\\n        11. just\\n        12. pays\\n        13. in\\n        14. rent.\\n        Total: 14 words.\\n\\n        Let\'s try to cut one more word to be safe.\\n        Line 1: My cat thinks the house is his.\\n        Line 2: He just refuses to pay rent.\\n        1. My\\n        2. cat\\n        3. t